      "Nyerere National Park, renamed from the Selous Game Reserve in 2019 in honour of Tanzania's founding president Julius Nyerere, is Africa's largest protected area at over 54,600 km² — roughly the size of Switzerland. A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1982, Nyerere's sheer scale and its network of lakes, channels, and the Rufiji River, Tanzania's largest river, give it a diversity of safari activities found nowhere else in the country.\n\nThe Rufiji River and its tributaries support one of Africa's densest concentrations of hippo and crocodile, along with substantial populations of elephant, buffalo, and — critically — one of the largest remaining populations of the endangered African wild dog on the continent. Lion and leopard are present in strong numbers, and the park's remoteness and low visitor density mean sightings here often feel more intimate and less rushed than in the busier northern parks.\n\nNyerere is unique among Tanzania's major parks in offering boat safaris along the Rufiji's channels and lakes, walking safaris led by armed rangers, and fly-camping under the stars, alongside conventional game drives — a genuine variety of ways to experience the bush that few other single destinations can match. Boat trips in particular offer close encounters with hippo pods and basking crocodiles, and access to birdlife along the water's edge that vehicles simply can't reach.\n\nThe dry season from June to October is prime time, when wildlife concentrates around the river system and boat safaris are at their best. Nyerere is reached by light aircraft from Dar es Salaam or Zanzibar rather than overland from the northern circuit, making it a natural pairing with a Zanzibar beach extension for travelers building a southern-circuit-and-coast itinerary distinct from the classic Serengeti route.",
