    description:
      "Volcanoes National Park, in Rwanda's far northwest, occupies the slopes of the Virunga Mountains — a chain of eight volcanoes straddling the borders of Rwanda, Uganda, and the Democratic Republic of Congo, five of which lie within Rwandan territory. This misty, bamboo-forested landscape is home to roughly half of the world's remaining mountain gorillas, making it one of the most significant conservation sites on the planet and the birthplace of modern gorilla conservation, thanks in large part to the work of primatologist Dian Fossey, who established her research camp here in 1967.\n\nThe park's population of habituated gorilla families allows visitors to trek through dense montane forest and spend a strictly regulated hour in the presence of a wild gorilla family — an experience widely described as one of the most profound wildlife encounters available anywhere on Earth, watching gorillas that share roughly 98% of human DNA go about their daily lives largely undisturbed by the visitors' presence. Beyond gorillas, Volcanoes is also one of only a few places on Earth to see golden monkeys, an endangered primate endemic to the Albertine Rift, found in the park's bamboo forest zones.\n\nTreks vary considerably in difficulty depending on which gorilla family has been assigned and where it is ranging that day — anywhere from a 30-minute walk to a strenuous multi-hour climb through steep, muddy terrain at altitudes above 2,500 metres, so a reasonable level of fitness is genuinely required. Beyond primate trekking, the park offers hikes to the summits of Bisoke and Karisimbi, visits to the Dian Fossey tomb and former research site, and access to crater lakes within the volcanic landscape.\n\nGorilla trekking is possible year-round, though the two dry seasons (June to September and December to February) offer easier, less muddy trekking conditions. Permits are strictly limited to protect the gorilla population and must be booked well in advance — often months ahead during peak season. Volcanoes sits roughly 450 km from Arusha via Kigali and is commonly combined with Rwanda's other parks or with a Tanzania itinerary for travelers building a broader East Africa primate-and-safari trip.",
