    description:
      "Amboseli National Park sits in the shadow of Mount Kilimanjaro, and on a clear morning the mountain's snow-capped summit rises directly above the park's open plains in one of Africa's most photographed backdrops — elephant herds framed against Africa's highest peak, despite Kilimanjaro itself lying just across the border in Tanzania.\n\nAmboseli is renowned for its exceptionally large elephant herds and for hosting some of the last \"tusker\" elephants in Africa — bulls whose tusks are so large they nearly reach the ground, a genetic trait now rare across the continent due to decades of poaching pressure elsewhere. The park's seasonal swamps, fed by underground rivers flowing from Kilimanjaro's glaciers, sustain wildlife even through the dry season, drawing lion, cheetah, buffalo, and over 600 recorded bird species to its permanent water sources.\n\nGame drives here center on Amboseli's open, largely treeless landscape — a legacy of past overgrazing and drought that, while ecologically complex, delivers outstanding visibility for photography and wildlife viewing. Observation Hill offers a rare vantage point for a sweeping panorama across the swamps and plains, and the Ol Tukai swamp area is consistently one of the most reliable spots in Kenya for close elephant encounters.\n\nAmboseli can be visited year-round, with the dry seasons (June to October and January to February) offering the clearest Kilimanjaro views and the most concentrated wildlife around the swamps. It sits roughly 270 km from Arusha and combines naturally with a Tanzania itinerary via the nearby border, as well as with the Masai Mara or Kenyan coast for a broader Kenya safari.",
