      "Kenya's Indian Ocean coastline stretches for over 500 kilometres from the Tanzanian border to Somalia, anchored by Mombasa — East Africa's oldest and largest port city — and offering a coastal culture and history distinct from anywhere else in the region, built on centuries of Swahili, Arab, Portuguese, and British influence.\n\nMombasa's Old Town and 16th-century Fort Jesus, a UNESCO World Heritage Site built by the Portuguese and fought over by Omani, British, and local forces for centuries, anchor a coastline where history is as much a draw as the beaches. North and south of the city, the coast unfolds into a string of distinct destinations: Diani's white sand and coral reefs to the south, Watamu and Malindi's marine parks further north, and the UNESCO-listed Swahili town of Lamu, reachable only by boat or small plane, where donkeys and dhows still outnumber cars.\n\nThe coast's coral reefs, protected within several marine national parks, support outstanding snorkeling and diving, along with encounters with whale sharks (seasonally), green and hawksbill turtles, and even dugongs in a few protected bays. Deep-sea fishing, traditional dhow sailing at sunset, and Swahili cooking experiences round out a coastal stay that offers as much cultural and culinary depth as beach time.\n\nThe coast is warmest and driest from December to March and July to October, avoiding the long and short rains; it is, for most visitors, the natural close to a Kenya safari itinerary — a short flight from Nairobi or the Masai Mara delivers travelers from the savanna to the ocean, and most stays run four to seven nights to properly experience both the beaches and Lamu's distinct Swahili heritage.",
